From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: Only SYSTEM mints administrators' tokens in a shared prefix

Multi-user debt D17. Upstream Wine's server creates tokens for anyone who
asks: every process there belongs to one Unix user who is its administrator
anyway. In a prefix shared between Unix users (patches 0001-0005) that is a
privilege escalation. Measured as a standard user, before this patch, with a
probe program:

  - a program whose manifest says requireAdministrator started with SYSTEM's
    token (S-1-5-18): the loader switches to the token's linked token, and
    create_linked_token answered with token_create_admin(), whose user is the
    server's own account -- SYSTEM;
  - GetTokenInformation(TokenLinkedToken) gave any program that token;
  - NtCreateToken made a token for SYSTEM with Administrators -- the server
    checks no privilege (SeCreateTokenPrivilege on Windows);
  - NtSetInformationProcess(ProcessWineGrantAdminToken) gave the calling
    process an administrator's token.

Any of them gives a standard user everything SYSTEM has on the Windows side:
HKLM, the service control manager, every object SYSTEM may open.

In a shared prefix, now:

  - a standard user's token has elevation type Default, as on Windows, and
    create_linked_token refuses standard users (STATUS_NO_SUCH_LOGON_SESSION);
    a requireAdministrator program runs as the user, unelevated, until the
    elevation broker (Stained Glass ADR 0012) gives it a real path;
  - create_token and grant_process_admin_token are for SYSTEM's own processes
    (the prefix owner, or root) only -- standing in for SeCreateTokenPrivilege,
    which only SYSTEM holds on Windows;
  - assigning a process a primary token for a different user needs the same
    (SeAssignPrimaryTokenPrivilege on Windows); a user may still assign a
    token of their own, e.g. a restricted copy.

Ordinary prefixes are unchanged: the checks apply only with the system-prefix
marker.

Tested with the probe (sg-session's sg-token-check): as a standard user, all
four attempts are denied, with and without a requireAdministrator manifest;
as SYSTEM, all are granted as before.

--- a/server/process.c
+++ b/server/process.c
@@ -722,7 +722,12 @@
             /* The user's hive has to exist before anything touches HKCU, and
              * this is the first moment we know which user that is. */
             sg_ensure_user_hive( process->peer_uid );
-            process->token = token_create_for_uid( TRUE, -1, TokenElevationTypeLimited,
+            /* Stained Glass OS (D17): only SYSTEM has a linked administrator
+             * token; a standard user's elevation type is Default, as on
+             * Windows, and elevation goes through the broker (ADR 0012). */
+            process->token = token_create_for_uid( TRUE, -1,
+                                                   (!process->peer_uid || process->peer_uid == sg_system_prefix_uid)
+                                                   ? TokenElevationTypeLimited : TokenElevationTypeDefault,
                                                    default_session_id, process->peer_uid );
         }
         else
@@ -1647,6 +1652,15 @@
 
